1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:05,520
Module 5, Video 5.6, Payments and Information Mediation as Named Crossings.

2
00:00:06,120 --> 00:00:08,020
Uh, that is our starting point for today's deep dive.

3
00:00:08,300 --> 00:00:17,920
Yeah, and you know, our mission today is really to help you, uh, the official running these systems in your ministry, because you need to understand how to prevent your services from becoming

4
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:21,120
fragmented, siloed, or, you know, costly to change.

5
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:29,420
Right. Costly to change. Because when you build a government digital service, how do you actually ensure it communicates seamlessly with other departments, uh, without just

6
00:00:29,740 --> 00:00:30,620
duplicating effort?

7
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:32,659
Well, before writing any code,

8
00:00:33,600 --> 00:00:37,280
you really have to map exactly how your system talks to the outside world.

9
00:00:37,860 --> 00:00:40,560
I mean, take a standard registration service in your program.

10
00:00:41,040 --> 00:00:43,100
Okay. So it, uh, it does three things, right?

11
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:46,460
It takes a fee, reads another body's register, and signs people in.

12
00:00:46,760 --> 00:00:47,540
Exactly. Hmm.

13
00:00:47,740 --> 00:00:52,280
And every single one of those actions leaves the service and has to come back.

14
00:00:52,980 --> 00:00:56,420
So a crossing is basically any flow that enters or leaves,

15
00:00:57,160 --> 00:01:04,580
and, uh, the GovStack registration specification expects all traffic into or out of a block to go through an information mediator.

16
00:01:04,959 --> 00:01:06,100
Or a secure gateway, right?

17
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:07,540
Right, a secure gateway.

18
00:01:07,940 --> 00:01:14,220
Because bypassing that information mediator is, uh, I mean, it's basically like building a private road that nobody else can check.

19
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:18,920
But why does the architecture require such a strict table for all these flows?

20
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:23,580
Uh, well, it's because the architecture must name four specific things for every crossing.

21
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:26,860
Number one, what crosses, and in which direction.

22
00:01:27,180 --> 00:01:27,880
Okay. Got it.

23
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:29,760
Number two, the body on the other side.

24
00:01:30,420 --> 00:01:35,160
Three, the block it passes through, and four, who on the other side actually agreed to it.

25
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:37,540
Wait, who on the other side agreed to it?

26
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:43,460
Let's, uh, let's apply this mapping framework to, you know, the most common crossing, which is collecting a fee.

27
00:01:44,020 --> 00:01:47,040
Right. So a service shouldn't just build its own payment screen.

28
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:48,200
It, it, um,

29
00:01:48,840 --> 00:01:51,280
it sends a request to the central payments block.

30
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:57,160
And the GovStack payment specification actually requires this request to carry a few specific things, right?

31
00:01:57,200 --> 00:02:02,220
Like, uh, the payer, payee, amount, currency, policy, and a transaction number?

32
00:02:02,700 --> 00:02:05,580
Yes, exactly. And the service only keeps the confirmation.

33
00:02:05,820 --> 00:02:08,020
I mean, it never touches the actual payment details.

34
00:02:08,900 --> 00:02:13,480
But what happens on day one of your program if the central payments block just isn't ready yet?

35
00:02:14,100 --> 00:02:16,280
Well, a manual backup is perfectly fine.

36
00:02:16,880 --> 00:02:22,720
I mean, an applicant pays into a bank, and then a finance officer manually records who, how much, and when.

37
00:02:23,720 --> 00:02:24,260
Oh, wow.

38
00:02:24,940 --> 00:02:26,840
So you literally just do it manually?

39
00:02:27,400 --> 00:02:29,100
It's honest, and it works.

40
00:02:29,860 --> 00:02:34,300
But crucially, the architecture still logs this as a crossing to move to the payments block later,

41
00:02:34,920 --> 00:02:38,040
and that ensures the upgrade is, you know, planned and budgeted for.

42
00:02:38,600 --> 00:02:39,380
That makes total sense.

43
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:47,720
Let's visualize this in practice using the fictional country of Progressa, specifically their Higher Education Quality Authority, uh, PHEQA.

44
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:53,240
Right. So PHEQA's registration service has four specific crossings.

45
00:02:53,960 --> 00:02:57,220
One, the fee out to the payments block and the confirmation back.

46
00:02:57,980 --> 00:03:03,280
Two, an institution's record out to MoEYS through Linkup, which PDGA operates.

47
00:03:03,660 --> 00:03:04,580
Okay, and the third one?

48
00:03:05,180 --> 00:03:08,060
Three is who signs in from PNIA.

49
00:03:08,760 --> 00:03:14,500
And four, ahead, a graduate's credential issued by PDCA into the learner's digital wallet.

50
00:03:15,220 --> 00:03:20,600
So why is it so vital that each row in the architecture table names a specific agreeing body?

51
00:03:21,440 --> 00:03:23,840
Well, because a crossing is ultimately a shared promise.

52
00:03:24,760 --> 00:03:26,320
You require four agreements here.

53
00:03:27,100 --> 00:03:29,840
One, the operator of the payments block confirms the fee.

54
00:03:30,640 --> 00:03:33,800
Two, PDGA confirms the linkup use. Right.

55
00:03:34,520 --> 00:03:37,000
Three, PNIA confirms the sign-in.

56
00:03:37,720 --> 00:03:40,140
And four, MoEYS confirms what it reads.

57
00:03:40,520 --> 00:03:45,220
So if the other side hasn't seen the row, it's a promise made on their behalf that will just fail its first test.

58
00:03:45,360 --> 00:03:50,360
Exactly. The rule of thumb here is never rebuild a block that a crossing can use.

59
00:03:51,480 --> 00:04:00,380
So to wrap up, in your ministry, name every crossing, the body on the other side, and the block it goes through to avoid rekeyed, duplicated systems locked into one supplier.

60
00:04:00,900 --> 00:04:02,600
Yes. That is exactly the goal.

